What is an outlier?

An outlier is a data point that deviates significantly from the rest of the observations in a dataset, to the point of arousing suspicion that it was generated by a different mechanism than the rest of the data.

Statistically far from the rest (the measurable part)

Possibly generated by a different process (the interpretive part)

Is an outlier always bad data?

no — sometimes an outlier is the most important data point in your dataset (a fraud case, a system failure, a high-value customer). Deleting it blindly can be worse than keeping it.


Cause	                                                     Example	                                                                                 What to do
Data entry error	                             Age entered as 200 instead of 20	                                                                    Fix or remove
Measurement/instrument error	                 Sensor glitch reads temperature as -999	                                                            Remove or treat as missing
Natural variation (genuine rare event)	       A billionaire's income in a salary dataset	                                                          Usually keep, maybe transform
Sampling error	                               Accidentally including a wrong population (e.g., a test account mixed into real user data)	           Remove
Novelty / the actual signal you care about	   Fraudulent transactions, network intrusions                                                        	Keep — this IS what you're trying to detect

Types of outliers 
Global (point) outliers: a single point far from all other data, considered against the entire dataset. Example: one salary of 5 crore among salaries of 5-10 lakh.
Contextual (conditional) outliers: normal in general, but abnormal in a specific context. Example: 35°C is a normal temperature in summer, but a huge outlier if recorded in winter — context (season) changes what "normal" means.
Collective outliers: a group of points, each individually looking fine, but together forming an anomalous pattern. Example: a sudden sustained spike in server requests over 10 minutes — no single request looks unusual, but the pattern does.
Most beginner treatment (IQR, Z-score) only catches global outliers. Catching contextual/collective outliers usually requires grouping by context first, or time-series/sequence-aware methods
